  "notes": "West leads the ♣Q against 4♥. West's opening club lead is won in the closed hand. South is in danger of losing a trick to the ♥K as well as 3 diamond tricks if West has the ♦A. North's spades will eventually provide a discard for 1 of South's diamonds.\n\nSouth should begin by drawing trump. The normal play with this combination would be to finesse against the ♥K. On this deal, however, South should play the ♥A on the first round of the suit as a safety play against East having a singleton ♥K.\n\nIf East follows low, South plays another round of trumps. If West wins the ♥K (the heart finesse would have worked) the contract is safe as West cannot effectively attack diamonds from his side of the table.\n\nIf East has the guarded ♥K, he will play back a diamond. South should play low on the first round of the suit. On the second round of diamonds, South should play the ♦K. He will succeed when East has the ♦A and when West has a doubleton ♦A.",
